./miniz/ChangeLog.md:80:The inflator now has a new failure status TINFL_STATUS_FAILED_CANNOT_MAKE_PROGRESS (-4). Previously, if the inflator was starved of bytes and could not make progress (because the input buffer was empty and the caller did not set the TINFL_FLAG_HAS_MORE_INPUT flag - say on truncated or corrupted compressed data stream) it would append all 0's to the input and try to soldier on. This is scary behavior if the caller didn't know when to stop accepting output (because it didn't know how much uncompressed data was expected, or didn't enforce a sane maximum). v1.16 will instead return TINFL_STATUS_FAILED_CANNOT_MAKE_PROGRESS immediately if it needs 1 or more bytes to make progress, the input buf is empty, and the caller has indicated that no more input is available. This is a "soft" failure, so you can call the inflator again with more input and it will try to continue, or you can give up and fail. This could be very useful in network streaming scenarios.
